+<!doctype html>
+<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Shared computational principles for language processing in humans and deep language models</title></head>
+<body>
+<article>
+<h1>Shared computational principles for language processing in humans and deep language models</h1>
+<div class="c-article-body"><section data-title="Results"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec5-section"><h2 class="c-article-section__title">Results</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec5-content">
+<p>The outstanding success of autoregressive (predictive) DLMs is striking from theoretical and practical perspectives because they have emerged from a very different scientific paradigm than traditional psycholinguist models<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 1" title="Linzen, T. &amp; Baroni, M. Syntactic structure from deep learning. Annu. Rev. Linguist. 7, 195–212 (2021)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR1" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e831">1</a></sup>. In traditional psycholinguistic approaches, human language is explained with interpretable models that combine symbolic elements (for example, nouns, verbs, adjectives and adverbs) with rule-based operations<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2" title="Chomsky, N. Syntactic structures. 
+                  https://doi.org/10.1515/9783112316009
+                  
+                 (1957)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR2" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e835">2</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 3" title="Jacobs, R. A. &amp; Rosenbaum, P. S. English Transformational Grammar (Blaisdell, 1968)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR3" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e838">3</a></sup>. In contrast, autoregressive DLMs learn language from real-world textual examples ‘in the wild’, with minimal or no explicit prior knowledge about language structure. Autoregressive DLMs do not parse words into parts of speech or apply explicit syntactic transformations. Rather, they learn to encode a sequence of words into a numerical vector, termed a contextual embedding, from which the model decodes the next word. After learning, the next-word prediction principle allows the generation of well-formed, novel, context-aware texts<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 1" title="Linzen, T. &amp; Baroni, M. Syntactic structure from deep learning. Annu. Rev. Linguist. 7, 195–212 (2021)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR1" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e842">1</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 4" title="Brown, T. B. et al. Language models are few-shot learners. Adv. Neural Inf. Process. Syst. 33, 1877–1901 (2020)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR4" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e845">4</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 5" title="Cho, W. S. et al. Towards coherent and cohesive long-form text generation. in Proceedings of the First Workshop on Narrative Understanding 
+                  https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/w19-2401
+                  
+                 (2019)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR5" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e848">5</a></sup>.</p>
+<p>Autoregressive DLMs have proven to be extremely effective in capturing the structure of language<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Yang, Z. et al. XLNet: generalized autoregressive pretraining for language understanding. in Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (eds. Wallach, H. et al.) 5753–5763 (Curran Associates, 2019)." href="#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e855">6</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Radford, A., Narasimhan, K., Salimans, T. &amp; Sutskever, I. Improving language understanding by generative pre-training. OpenAI Blog (2018)." href="#ref-CR7" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e855_1">7</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Radford, A. et al. Language models are unsupervised multitask learners. OpenAI Blog (2019)." href="#ref-CR8" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e855_2">8</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 9" title="Rosset, C. Turing-nlg: A 17-billion-parameter language model by microsoft. Microsoft Blog (2019)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR9" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e858">9</a></sup>. It is unclear, however, if the core computational principles of autoregressive DLMs are related to the way the human brain processes language. Past research has leveraged language models and machine learning to extract semantic representation in the brain<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Pereira, F. et al. Toward a universal decoder of linguistic meaning from brain activation. Nat. Commun. 9, 963 (2018)." href="#ref-CR10" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e862">10</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Makin, J. G., Moses, D. A. &amp; Chang, E. F. Machine translation of cortical activity to text with an encoder–decoder framework. Nat. Neurosci. 23, 575–582 (2020)." href="#ref-CR11" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e862_1">11</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Schwartz, D. et al. Inducing brain-relevant bias in natural language processing models. in Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (eds. Wallach, H. et al.) 14123–14133 (Curran Associates, 2019)." href="#ref-CR12" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e862_2">12</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Gauthier, J. &amp; Levy, R. Linking artificial and human neural representations of language. in Proceedings of the 2019 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing and the 9th International Joint Conference on Natural Language Processing 
+                  https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/d19-1050
+                  
+                 (2019)." href="#ref-CR13" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e862_3">13</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Donhauser, P. W. &amp; Baillet, S. Two distinct neural timescales for predictive speech processing. Neuron 105, 385–393 (2020)." href="#ref-CR14" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e862_4">14</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Jain, S. &amp; Huth, A. G. Incorporating context into language encoding models for fMRI. in Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 
+                  https://doi.org/10.1101/327601
+                  
+                 (2018)." href="#ref-CR15" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e862_5">15</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Schrimpf, M. et al. Artificial neural networks accurately predict language processing in the Brain. Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory 
+                  https://doi.org/10.1101/2020.06.26.174482
+                  
+                 (2020)." href="#ref-CR16" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e862_6">16</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Caucheteux, C., Gramfort, A. &amp; King, J. -R. GPT-2’s activations predict the degree of semantic comprehension in the human brain. 
+                  https://doi.org/10.1101/2021.04.20.440622
+                  
+                 (2021)." href="#ref-CR17" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e862_7">17</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 18" title="Athanasiou, N., Iosif, E. &amp; Potamianos, A. Neural activation semantic models: computational lexical semantic models of localized neural activations. in Proceedings of the 27th International Conference on Computational Linguistics 2867–2878 (Association for Computational Linguistics, 2018)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR18" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e865">18</a></sup>. But such studies did not view autoregressive DLMs as feasible cognitive models for how the human brain codes language. In contrast, recent theoretical papers argue that there are fundamental connections between DLMs and how the brain processes language<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 1" title="Linzen, T. &amp; Baroni, M. Syntactic structure from deep learning. Annu. Rev. Linguist. 7, 195–212 (2021)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR1" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e869">1</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 19" title="McClelland, J. L., Hill, F., Rudolph, M., Baldridge, J. &amp; Schütze, H. Placing language in an integrated understanding system: next steps toward human-level performance in neural language models. Proc. Natl Acad. Sci. USA 117, 25966–25974 (2020)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR19" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e872">19</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 20" title="Hasson, U., Nastase, S. A. &amp; Goldstein, A. Direct fit to nature: an evolutionary perspective on biological and artificial neural networks. Neuron 105, 416–434 (2020)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR20" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e875">20</a></sup>.</p>
+<div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-1" data-title="Shared computational principles between the brain and autoregressive deep language models in processing natural language."><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig1" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 1: Shared computational principles between the brain and autoregressive deep language models in processing natural language.</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41593-022-01026-4/MediaObjects/41593_2022_1026_Fig1_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-1-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41593-022-01026-4/MediaObjects/41593_2022_1026_Fig1_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 1: Shared computational principles between the brain and autoregressive deep language models in processing natural language." loading="lazy" width="685" height="581"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4/figures/1" data-track-dest="link:Figure1 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 1" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-1-desc"><p>For each sequence of words in the text, GPT-2 generates a contextual embedding (blue), which is used to assign probabilities to the identity of the next word (green box). Next, GPT-2 uses its pre-onset predictions to calculate its surprise level (that is, error signal) when the next word is revealed (purple box). The minimization of the surprise is the mechanism by which GPT-2 is trained to generate well-formed outputs. Each colored box and arrow represents the relationship between a given computational principle of the autoregressive DLM and the neural responses. The green boxes represent that both the brain and autoregressive DLMs are engaged in context-dependent prediction of the upcoming word before word onset. The green arrow indicates that we take the actual predictions from GPT-2 (for example, the top-one prediction ‘ocean’ for the example sentence ‘our story begins deep in the…’) to model the neural responses before word onset (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#Fig4">4</a>). The purple boxes represent that both the brain and autoregressive DLMs are engaged in the assessment of their predictions after word onset. The purple arrow indicates that we take the actual perceived next word (‘rainforest’ in our example), as well as GPT-2’s surprise level for the perceived word (cross-entropy) to model the neural responses after word onset (Figs. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#Fig4">4b</a> and <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#Fig5">5b</a>). The blue boxes represent that both the brain and autoregressive DLMs use contextual embeddings to represent words. The blue arrow indicates that we take the contextual embeddings from GPT-2 to model the neural responses (Figs. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#Fig6">6</a> and <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#Fig8">8</a>). We argue here that, although the internal word-processing mechanisms are not the same for the brain and DLMs, they do share three core computational principles: (1) continuous context-dependent next-word prediction before word onset; (2) reliance on the pre-onset prediction to calculate post-word-onset surprise; and finally, (3) context-specific representation of meaning.</p></div></div></figure></div>
+<p>In agreement with this theoretical perspective, we provide empirical evidence that the human brain processes incoming speech similarly to an autoregressive DLM (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#Fig1">1</a>). In particular, the human brain and autoregressive DLMs share three computational principles: (1) both are engaged in continuous context-dependent next-word prediction before word onset; (2) both match pre-onset predictions to the incoming word to induce post-onset surprise (that is, prediction-error signals); (3) both represent words using contextual embeddings. The main contribution of this study is the provision of direct evidence for the continuous engagement of the brain in next-word prediction before word onset (computational principle 1). In agreement with recent publications<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 14" title="Donhauser, P. W. &amp; Baillet, S. Two distinct neural timescales for predictive speech processing. Neuron 105, 385–393 (2020)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR14" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e885">14</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 16" title="Schrimpf, M. et al. Artificial neural networks accurately predict language processing in the Brain. Cold Spring Harbor Laboratory 
+                  https://doi.org/10.1101/2020.06.26.174482
+                  
+                 (2020)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR16" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e888">16</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Heilbron, M., Armeni, K., Schoffelen, J.-M., Hagoort, P. &amp; de Lange, F. P. A hierarchy of linguistic predictions during natural language comprehension. Preprint at bioRxiv 
+                  https://doi.org/10.1101/2020.12.03.410399
+                  
+                 (2020)." href="#ref-CR21" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e891">21</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Weissbart, H., Kandylaki, K. D. &amp; Reichenbach, T. Cortical tracking of surprisal during continuous speech comprehension. J. Cogn. Neurosci. 32, 155–166 (2020)." href="#ref-CR22" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e891_1">22</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Frank, S. L., Otten, L. J., Galli, G. &amp; Vigliocco, G. The ERP response to the amount of information conveyed by words in sentences. Brain Lang. 140, 1–11 (2015)." href="#ref-CR23" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e891_2">23</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 24" title="Caucheteux, C., Gramfort, A. &amp; King, J.-R. GPT-2’s activations predict the degree of semantic comprehension in the human brain. Preprint at bioRxiv 
+                  https://doi.org/10.1101/2021.04.20.440622
+                  
+                 (2021)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR24" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e894">24</a></sup>, we also provide neural evidence in support of computational principles 2 and 3.</p>
+<div class="c-article-section__figure js-c-reading-companion-figures-item" data-test="figure" data-container-section="figure" id="figure-2" data-title="Behavioral assessment of the human ability to predict forthcoming words in a natural context."><figure><figcaption><b id="Fig2" class="c-article-section__figure-caption" data-test="figure-caption-text">Fig. 2: Behavioral assessment of the human ability to predict forthcoming words in a natural context.</b></figcaption><div class="c-article-section__figure-content"><div class="c-article-section__figure-item"><picture class="c-article-section__figure-picture"><source type="image/webp" srcset="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41593-022-01026-4/MediaObjects/41593_2022_1026_Fig2_HTML.png?as=webp"><img aria-describedby="figure-2-desc" src="//media.springernature.com/lw685/springer-static/image/art%3A10.1038%2Fs41593-022-01026-4/MediaObjects/41593_2022_1026_Fig2_HTML.png" alt="Fig. 2: Behavioral assessment of the human ability to predict forthcoming words in a natural context." loading="lazy" width="685" height="593"></picture><div class="c-article-section__figure-link"><a class="c-article__pill-button" data-test="article-link" data-track="click" data-track-label="button" data-track-action="view figure" href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4/figures/2" data-track-dest="link:Figure2 Full size image" aria-label="Full size image figure 2" rel="nofollow"><span>Full size image</span><svg width="16" height="16" focusable="false" role="img" aria-hidden="true" class="u-icon"><use xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="#icon-eds-i-chevron-right-small"></use></svg></a></div></div><div class="c-article-section__figure-description" data-test="bottom-caption" id="figure-2-desc"><p><b>a</b>, The stimulus was transcribed for the behavioral experiment. <b>b</b>, A ten-word sliding window was presented in each trial, and participants were asked to type their prediction of the next word. Once entered, the correct word is presented, and the window slides forward by one word. <b>c</b>, For each word, we calculated the proportion of participants that predicted the forthcoming word correctly. <b>d</b>, Human predictability scores across words. <b>e</b>, Human predictability scores versus GPT-2’s predictability scores for each upcoming word in the podcast. <b>f</b>, Match between assigned probability for humans and GPT-2 and the actual accuracy for their top-one predictions. <b>g</b>, Correlation between human predictions and GPT-2 predictions (as reported in <b>d</b>) for different context window lengths ranging from 2 to 1,024 preceding tokens (blue). Correlation between human predictions and 2- to 5-gram model predictions (orange).</p></div></div></figure></div>
+<p>The constant engagement in next-word prediction before word onset is the bedrock objective of autoregressive DLMs<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Yang, Z. et al. XLNet: generalized autoregressive pretraining for language understanding. in Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (eds. Wallach, H. et al.) 5753–5763 (Curran Associates, 2019)." href="#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e940">6</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Radford, A., Narasimhan, K., Salimans, T. &amp; Sutskever, I. Improving language understanding by generative pre-training. OpenAI Blog (2018)." href="#ref-CR7" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e940_1">7</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Radford, A. et al. Language models are unsupervised multitask learners. OpenAI Blog (2019)." href="#ref-CR8" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e940_2">8</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 9" title="Rosset, C. Turing-nlg: A 17-billion-parameter language model by microsoft. Microsoft Blog (2019)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR9" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e943">9</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 25" title="Lewis, M. et al. BART: denoising sequence-to-sequence pretraining for natural language generation, translation, and comprehension. Preprint at 
+                  https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.13461
+                  
+                 (2019)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR25" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e946">25</a></sup>. Similarly, the claim that the brain is constantly engaged in predicting the incoming input is fundamental to numerous predictive coding theories<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Huang, Y. &amp; Rao, R. P. N. Predictive coding. Wiley Interdiscip. Rev. Cogn. Sci. 2, 580–593 (2011)." href="#ref-CR26" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e950">26</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Lupyan, G. &amp; Clark, A. Words and the world: predictive coding and the language–perception–cognition interface. Curr. Dir. Psychol. Sci. 24, 279–284 (2015)." href="#ref-CR27" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e950_1">27</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Barron, H. C., Auksztulewicz, R. &amp; Friston, K. Prediction and memory: a predictive coding account. Prog. Neurobiol. 192, 101821 (2020)." href="#ref-CR28" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e950_2">28</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Goldstein, A., Rivlin, I., Goldstein, A., Pertzov, Y. &amp; Hassin, R. R. Predictions from masked motion with and without obstacles. PLoS ONE 15, e0239839 (2020)." href="#ref-CR29" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e950_3">29</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 30" title="Clark, A. Whatever next? Predictive brains, situated agents, and the future of cognitive science. Behav. Brain Sci. 36, 181–204 (2013)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR30" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e953">30</a></sup>. However, even after decades of research, behavioral and neural evidence for the brain’s propensity to predict upcoming words before they are perceived during natural language processing has remained indirect. On the behavioral level, the ability to predict upcoming words has typically been tested with highly controlled sentence stimuli (that is, the cloze procedure<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Taylor, W. L. ‘Cloze Procedure’: a new tool for measuring readability. Journal Q. 30, 415–433 (1953)." href="#ref-CR31" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e957">31</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="Kliegl, R., Nuthmann, A. &amp; Engbert, R. Tracking the mind during reading: the influence of past, present, and future words on fixation durations. J. Exp. Psychol. Gen. 135, 12–35 (2006)." href="#ref-CR32" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e957_1">32</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 33" title="Laurinavichyute, A. K., Sekerina, I. A., Alexeeva, S., Bagdasaryan, K. &amp; Kliegl, R. Russian sentence corpus: benchmark measures of eye movements in reading in Russian. Behav. Res. Methods 51, 1161–1178 (2019)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR33" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e960">33</a></sup>). Thus, we still do not know how accurate listeners’ predictions are in open-ended natural contexts. The first section of the paper provides new behavioral evidence that humans can predict forthcoming words in a natural context with remarkable accuracy, and that, given a sufficient context window, next-word predictions in humans and an autoregressive DLM (GPT-2)<sup><a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 8" title="Radford, A. et al. Language models are unsupervised multitask learners. OpenAI Blog (2019)." href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#ref-CR8" id="ref-link-section-d72798114e964">8</a></sup> match. On the neuronal level, we provide new evidence that the brain is spontaneously engaged in next-word prediction before word onset during the processing of natural language. These findings provide the missing evidence that the brain, like autoregressive DLMs, is constantly involved in next-word prediction before word onset as it processes natural language (Fig. <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="figure anchor" href="/articles/s41593-022-01026-4#Fig1">1</a>).</p>
+</div></div></section></div>
+</article>
